  "live_records": {
    "q-26056": {
      "dom_id": "step-q26056",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "The point $Q$ with coordinates $(-1,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)$ and the point $R$ with coordinates $(-1,-2)$ lies on the curve $y=g(x).$ If the curve $y=g(x)$ is related to $y=f(x)$ via the transformation $g(x)=f(x)+k$, then what is the value of $k?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that the point $R(-1,-2)$ lies $3$ units below the point $Q(-1,1).$ So, the graph of $y=g(x)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by shifting it $3$ units down. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14228870/assets/7d470919b374a5796b49ff81f893f37f5bb76afd4546a25459259f3b6339529b.png)\n\nAs a result, $k=-3.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
