    "q-86632": {
      "dom_id": "step-q86632",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "The point $Q$ with coordinates $(-2,1)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)$ and the point $R$ with coordinates $(-2,-1)$ lies on the curve $y=g(x).$ If the curve $y=g(x)$ is related to $y=f(x)$ via the transformation $g(x)=f(x)+k$, then what is the value of $k?$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that the point $R(-2,-1)$ lies $2$ units below the point $Q(-2,1).$ So, the graph of $y=g(x)$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by shifting it $2$ units down. \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14228870/assets/158488a446217332d0b70d2e6b116bc1e17e7e5b205ff5ebe4289a75ef05fa05.png)\n\nAs a result, $k=-2.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-86632-a",
              "type": "math",
              "value": "1",
              "html": "<mjx-container class=\"MathJax CtxtMenu_Attached_0\" jax=\"CHTML\" tabindex=\"0\" ctxtmenu_counter=\"55\" style=\"font-size: 119.6%; position: relative;\"><mjx-math class=\"MJX-TEX\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><mjx-mn class=\"mjx-n\"><mjx-c class=\"mjx-c31\"></mjx-c></mjx-mn></mjx-math><mjx-assistive-mml unselectable=\"on\" display=\"inline\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn></math></mjx-assistive-mml></mjx-container>"
            },
